## Proof

Let $S_{\mathbb{N}}$ denote the set of all bijections $\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ (the symmetric group on $\mathbb{N}$). We show $|S_{\mathbb{N}}| = 2^{\aleph_0}$, the cardinality of the continuum.

### Upper bound: $|S_{\mathbb{N}}| \leq 2^{\aleph_0}$

Every bijection is in particular a function, so
$$S_{\mathbb{N}} \subseteq \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}.$$
The set of all functions $\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ has cardinality
$$|\mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}| = \aleph_0^{\aleph_0}.$$
Since $2 \leq \aleph_0 \leq 2^{\aleph_0}$, cardinal exponentiation gives
$$2^{\aleph_0} \;\leq\; \aleph_0^{\aleph_0} \;\leq\; (2^{\aleph_0})^{\aleph_0} = 2^{\aleph_0\cdot\aleph_0} = 2^{\aleph_0}.$$
Hence $\aleph_0^{\aleph_0} = 2^{\aleph_0}$, and therefore
$$|S_{\mathbb{N}}| \leq 2^{\aleph_0}.$$

### Lower bound: $|S_{\mathbb{N}}| \geq 2^{\aleph_0}$

We construct an injection $\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{N}} \hookrightarrow S_{\mathbb{N}}$.

Partition $\mathbb{N}$ into consecutive pairs $P_k = \{2k,\, 2k+1\}$ for $k=0,1,2,\dots$. For each binary sequence $b=(b_0,b_1,b_2,\dots)\in\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{N}}$, define $\sigma_b:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ by acting on each pair independently:
$$\sigma_b(2k) = \begin{cases} 2k & b_k=0 \\ 2k+1 & b_k=1 \end{cases}, \qquad \sigma_b(2k+1) = \begin{cases} 2k+1 & b_k=0 \\ 2k & b_k=1 \end{cases}.$$

**$\sigma_b$ is a bijection.** On each pair $P_k$ it is either the identity (when $b_k=0$) or the transposition $(2k\;\; 2k{+}1)$ (when $b_k=1$). It is therefore a product of disjoint transpositions/identities on the disjoint pairs $\{P_k\}$, which is a permutation of $\mathbb{N}$.

**The map $b\mapsto\sigma_b$ is injective.** If $b\neq b'$, pick $k$ with $b_k\neq b'_k$. Then $\sigma_b(2k)\neq \sigma_{b'}(2k)$, so $\sigma_b\neq\sigma_{b'}$.

Thus $\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{N}}$ embeds into $S_{\mathbb{N}}$, giving
$$|S_{\mathbb{N}}| \geq |\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{N}}| = 2^{\aleph_0}.$$

### Conclusion

Combining both bounds,
$$\boxed{|S_{\mathbb{N}}| = 2^{\aleph_0}}.$$

The set of all bijections from $\mathbb{N}$ to itself has the cardinality of the continuum.

### PROOF COMPLETE
